1
00:00:00,100 --> 00:00:02,230
Before an astronaut ever sets foot on the

2
00:00:02,243 --> 00:00:05,100
Lunar South Pole, or a rover cracks Martian

3
00:00:05,113 --> 00:00:08,722
soil, every habitat, every rover wheel, every

4
00:00:08,735 --> 00:00:11,299
spacesuit joint has to survive a brutal dress

5
00:00:11,312 --> 00:00:12,421
rehearsal on Earth.

6
00:00:12,792 --> 00:00:16,211
We're the team behind PTAE, and for the 2026

7
00:00:16,223 --> 00:00:19,476
NASA Space Apps Challenge, we asked one question:

8
00:00:19,973 --> 00:00:22,116
how does NASA actually know an Earth test

9
00:00:22,129 --> 00:00:24,272
site behaves like the Moon or Mars?

10
00:00:24,642 --> 00:00:27,193
Today, that answer is mostly a guess, "it's

11
00:00:27,206 --> 00:00:30,216
a desert, so it's basically Mars." We built

12
00:00:30,229 --> 00:00:32,346
something that replaces the guess with a number.

13
00:00:32,800 --> 00:00:35,172
Mission planners already know a handful of

14
00:00:35,185 --> 00:00:38,795
famous analog sites, Devon Island, the Atacama

15
00:00:38,808 --> 00:00:41,308
Desert, Hawaii's lava fields.

16
00:00:41,679 --> 00:00:44,255
But those were chosen decades ago by intuition,

17
00:00:44,574 --> 00:00:45,467
not measurement.

18
00:00:45,837 --> 00:00:48,949
As Artemis and Mars missions get more ambitious,

19
00:00:49,293 --> 00:00:50,696
that's not good enough anymore.

20
00:00:51,066 --> 00:00:53,592
A lunar south-pole lander needs terrain with

21
00:00:53,605 --> 00:00:56,564
the right slope distribution for a safe touchdown.

22
00:00:56,934 --> 00:00:59,536
A Mars rover needs the right ruggedness so

23
00:00:59,548 --> 00:01:01,130
its wheels don't get trapped.

24
00:01:01,500 --> 00:01:03,579
Pick the wrong analog, and you find out your

25
00:01:03,592 --> 00:01:06,003
equipment fails only after it's already on

26
00:01:06,015 --> 00:01:08,605
another world, when it's too late and too

27
00:01:08,617 --> 00:01:09,765
expensive to fix.

28
00:01:10,135 --> 00:01:12,444
The real problem isn't a lack of candidate

29
00:01:12,457 --> 00:01:13,362
sites on Earth.

30
00:01:13,732 --> 00:01:16,232
It's the lack of a quantitative, repeatable

31
00:01:16,245 --> 00:01:18,400
way to score them against the Moon and Mars

32
00:01:18,413 --> 00:01:20,339
terrain data we already have.

33
00:01:20,800 --> 00:01:22,280
So we built PTAE.

34
00:01:22,651 --> 00:01:25,304
Point it at a target, the lunar south pole

35
00:01:25,316 --> 00:01:28,505
region, or Jezero Crater on Mars, and it does

36
00:01:28,518 --> 00:01:29,513
the science for you.

37
00:01:29,883 --> 00:01:33,071
Current results rank 20 Earth sites
using real AWS Terrain Tiles DEMs,

38
00:01:33,378 --> 00:01:36,273
with LOLA for the Moon target
and MOLA for the Mars target.

39
00:01:36,286 --> 00:01:39,385
Our Analog Compatibility Index
compares their terrain statistics.

40
00:01:39,398 --> 00:01:42,217
The synthetic demo is a separate
five-site illustration.

41
00:01:42,587 --> 00:01:45,776
For the lunar south-pole target,
Teide leads the 20-site ranking

42
00:01:45,788 --> 00:01:48,607
at 87.22 percent; Mount Etna
follows at 86.79 percent.

43
00:01:49,092 --> 00:01:51,694
These are terrain similarities,
not proof of mission suitability.

44
00:01:52,140 --> 00:01:54,474
Now switch to the current

45
00:01:54,844 --> 00:01:57,128
Mars Jezero terrain ranking.

46
00:01:57,140 --> 00:02:00,533
Kilauea leads at 55.84 percent,

47
00:02:00,546 --> 00:02:00,954
with

48
00:02:01,324 --> 00:02:04,156
Craters of the Moon second at 54.99 percent.

49
00:02:04,168 --> 00:02:07,242
A separate historical five-site study

50
00:02:07,255 --> 00:02:09,844
used real LOLA and Askja, plus four synthetic sites.

51
00:02:10,214 --> 00:02:13,684
Its leader changed across 5, 10, and 15 km windows.

52
00:02:13,773 --> 00:02:16,337
The new all-real crop study uses existing windows only.

53
00:02:16,350 --> 00:02:19,309
Mars and Earth still differ in resolution and physical footprint.

54
00:02:19,800 --> 00:02:22,938
Current results use 20 real Earth DEMs,

55
00:02:22,950 --> 00:02:26,063
from AWS Terrain Tiles, with LOLA

56
00:02:26,420 --> 00:02:29,456
and MOLA target DEMs for Moon and Mars.

57
00:02:29,774 --> 00:02:32,096
A separate five-site synthetic illustration

58
00:02:32,109 --> 00:02:33,002
provides a self-contained example,

59
00:02:33,372 --> 00:02:36,281
not the current dashboard rankings.

60
00:02:36,293 --> 00:02:39,214
The prepared arrays are already included;

61
00:02:39,253 --> 00:02:43,028
the dashboard reads checked-in JSON results.

62
00:02:43,041 --> 00:02:46,076
These terrain similarities do not prove

63
00:02:46,089 --> 00:02:48,628
mission suitability.

64
00:02:48,997 --> 00:02:51,689
That's our next step: connect PTAE to Google

65
00:02:51,702 --> 00:02:54,189
Earth Engine for a continuous global scan,

66
00:02:54,622 --> 00:02:57,403
and layer in mineralogy from CRISM and ASTER

67
00:02:57,480 --> 00:03:00,439
so we're matching chemistry, not just terrain

68
00:03:00,452 --> 00:03:00,872
shape.

69
00:03:01,242 --> 00:03:04,469
Our ask is simple, mission planners and analog-site

70
00:03:04,482 --> 00:03:07,084
researchers, give us your candidate sites

71
00:03:07,097 --> 00:03:09,724
and your target metrics, and PTAE will tell

72
00:03:09,737 --> 00:03:12,378
you, with a number, how good a match you've

73
00:03:12,403 --> 00:03:13,334
really found.

74
00:03:13,704 --> 00:03:15,872
Because the next crew that walks on the Moon

75
00:03:15,885 --> 00:03:17,913
deserves to have trained on the right ground

76
00:03:17,926 --> 00:03:18,449
first.

77
00:03:18,819 --> 00:03:20,847
We're the team behind PTAE, thank you.
